diagnosis_text:
The network is currently healthy and the partner prefix is being advertised and routed correctly. From the host perspective, end-to-end reachability from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) is normal: topology-wide probes show 0% packet loss and ~1.3 ms RTT. On router1, the routing table contains a BGP-learned route to 200.1.1.0/24: “B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, eth0,” proving router1 has an active path to the partner network. BGP on both routers is established and exchanging prefixes: router1’s “show ip bgp summary” shows the neighbor 193.10.11.2 in Established state with 1 prefix received; router2 shows the reciprocal session with 1 prefix received and its connected 200.1.1.0/24 present locally. Router2’s own RIB confirms 200.1.1.0/24 is directly connected on eth1.

Mechanism: Because router2 is advertising 200.1.1.0/24 and router1 installed it, traffic from pc1 is forwarded via router1→router2 as expected, resulting in successful pings.

Localization: No failing interface or daemon was found on router1 or router2; BGP sessions are up on the inter-router link (router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0).

Root cause: No persistent fault is present; the reported issue is not reproducible at the time of testing.

Corrective fix: No action required. Do not restart any device. If the problem recurs, check FRR logs for transient BGP flaps; otherwise, treat as a cleared incident.
